<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What a witness claims about a corpus of questions.

  Copyright (c) 2026 Kenny Flegal. Licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0.

  ⚠️ PROVISIONAL NAMESPACE URI, same as the base schema.

  ⛔⛔ SCOPE, AND IT IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS. Three objects exist in an assertion
  system: the QUESTION, the CLAIM about it, and the RUN that happened. This schema
  carries the first two and DELIBERATELY NOT THE THIRD.

  ⭐⭐ AND A FOURTH ARRIVED WITH `dependence`, WHICH IS THE CLAIM ROW POINTED OUTWARD: a
  claim about TWO OTHER PARTIES' FILINGS, made by somebody who filed neither. It is here
  and not in the base schema for the reason that decides everything in this file: a
  filing is attestable by the party that files it, so an observation spanning two filings
  belongs to the third party who read both. See Dependence.

    the question   stays in each corpus. Datetime formats, facility cases and plan
                   claims are different subjects; a shared corpus would be a shared
                   subject. Only its PROVENANCE is unified here, as Citation.
    the claim      ⭐ IS THIS SCHEMA'S REASON TO EXIST. It crosses organisations: an
                   accountant's answers to a corpus ARE a coverage file, and they
                   should not have to run anybody's code to send one.
    the run        ⭐ HERE, BUT ONLY IN ITS PROMOTED FORM, AND THE DISTINCTION IS
                   LOAD-BEARING. A runner's working ledger is a local, append-only
                   file answering "when did this go red?", machine-local, growing
                   forever, and NEVER a shareable artifact or a gate predicate. This
                   schema does not describe it. What it describes is a run PROMOTED
                   TO EVIDENCE: the extract a report cites, dated, frozen and sent.
                   ⛔ Emitting the working ledger in this format would be a category
                   error: it would look citable and would not be.

  ⛔ AND NO RUNNER IS DESCRIBED. Unification here is BY CONFORMANCE, NOT BY
  DEPENDENCY: a shared vocabulary plus a test per runner that it conforms, never a
  library everything imports. Two models that share a code path cannot corroborate
  each other, so the whole point is that a witness imports nothing from here.
-->
<xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
           xmlns:asrt="https://example.invalid/assertion/1.0"
           xmlns:pm="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0"
           targetNamespace="https://example.invalid/assertion/1.0"
           elementFormDefault="qualified"
           version="0.3.0">

  <xs:import namespace="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0"
             schemaLocation="process-modulus.xsd"/>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       ANSWER: what one witness says about one question.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Answer">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ BOTH ANSWERS THAT CARRY A VALUE CARRY A **BORROWED** ONE, AND THAT IS THE
      WHOLE CORRECTION THIS SCHEMA MAKES. A refusal code and a chart position are
      both drawn from a list SOMEBODY ELSE PUBLISHES, and a witness under a different
      regime draws from a different list.

        holds      Recognised, and WHERE: a position in a chart of accounts. The
                   chart is the taxonomy. A US chart and a Portuguese one do not
                   share a single code, so `1200` alone names nothing.
        refuses    ⭐ THE DELIVERABLE. A TYPED refusal, drawn from the coding pack
                   the accountants hold. That pack is the authority, not this
                   schema, and not any witness's implementation of it.
        cannotAsk  ⛔ NEITHER A FAILURE NOR A REFUSAL. The witness has no opinion on
                   this kind of question. A standards model asked about staffing
                   policy answers this and is right to.

      ⚠️ WHAT THIS REPLACES, AND WHY IT WAS A FORK WAITING TO HAPPEN. The
      implementation this came from held the six refusal codes as a compiled array of
      bare strings, with a comment saying the authority was the accountants' pack.
      **A comment naming an authority beside a hard-coded copy of its list is a fork
      that has not drifted yet.** Add a second regime and there are two bad options:
      widen the array and lose which word belongs to whom, or fork it.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE SHARED-VOCABULARY PROPERTY SURVIVES THE CHANGE, WHICH IS THE POINT.
      Two answers are comparable row for row when they cite the SAME taxonomy, and
      now two answers under DIFFERENT taxonomies are legible as different rather than
      silently incomparable. That is strictly more than the array could say.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="holds"     type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="refuses"   type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="cannotAsk" type="asrt:Nothing"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Nothing">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      An answer with no payload. It exists because XSD 1.0 has no empty-branch choice,
      and because `cannotAsk` genuinely carries nothing: inventing a field for it
      would invite somebody to fill one in.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       CLAIMED: how much of a question a witness says it answers.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="Claimed">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      HOW MUCH OF THE QUESTION THIS WITNESS CLAIMS TO ANSWER.

      ⛔⛔⛔ THIS WAS AN `xs:boolean` NAMED `complete`, THE ONLY ONE IN EITHER SCHEMA,
      AND IT WAS THE LAST SURVIVOR OF A PATTERN THIS PROJECT HAS NOW CAUGHT FIVE TIMES:
      A TWO-VALUED ENCODING SURVIVES REVIEW BECAUSE BOTH OF ITS VALUES ARE CORRECT.
      `true` was right, `false` was right, and the missing third value was the one that
      mattered — nothing in a boolean field points at what it cannot say.

        full     the witness answers the question and nothing is left over. The old
                 `true`, unchanged
        partial  ⭐⭐ THE VALUE THAT HAD NO ENCODING. The witness answers part of the
                 question and leaves part of it open. A framework that codes the
                 transacted half of an absorbed cost and has no position for the rest
                 is in this state, and it is the MOST INTERESTING state a witness can
                 be in, because the gap is exactly what a corpus about absorbed
                 quantities is asking about
        none     the witness answers no part of it, on purpose. A standards model with
                 no opinion about staffing policy

      ⭐ `false` WAS CARRYING `partial` AND `none` AT ONCE, and the two produce opposite
      readings of the same file: `none` says the question is outside this witness's
      subject, `partial` says it is inside and half-covered. A report that merges them
      cannot tell a witness that declines from a witness that falls short.

      ⭐⭐ AND IT SHARPENS `Verdict/notable` WITHOUT ADDING A MEMBER. `notable` already
      means the witness answered beyond what it claimed; with three values the claim is
      GRADED, so a `partial` witness that answers fully is notable in exactly the same
      sense a `none` witness that answers at all is. One definition, two more ways to
      reach it. See Verdict.

      ⚠️ NOT AN AbsenceReason, AND THE LINE IS THE SAME ONE `Verdict` DRAWS. `pm:Absence`
      says why a DOCUMENT has no value. This says how much of a QUESTION an ANSWERER
      takes on. `none` appears in both vocabularies meaning "somebody looked and the
      answer is nothing", which is the shared root and not a shared type.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="full"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="partial"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="none"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       VERDICT: how an answer stood against the corpus. CONTRIBUTED, and
       regime-neutral: it is about the corpus and the witness, not about
       any accounting framework.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="Verdict">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      agreed     Claimed, and the answer matches the corpus (or its declared
                 exception).

      diverged   Claimed, and the answer differs with no exception. ⛔ THERE ARE
                 EXACTLY THREE LEGITIMATE RESPONSES: fix the witness, fix the case,
                 or declare the exception. Editing the expected answer to match what
                 the witness happens to do is none of them: it turns the corpus into
                 a snapshot of current behaviour and the trial measures nothing.

      notable    ⭐⭐ ANSWERED BEYOND WHAT WAS CLAIMED. Never a failure, and usually the
                 most valuable line in a report: a witness admitted something we assumed
                 it must refuse, so either the model is stronger than we thought or a
                 guard is not guarding.
                 ⭐ THE DEFINITION USED TO READ "not claimed, and the witness answered
                 anyway", WHICH WAS THE SAME VERDICT DESCRIBED THROUGH A BOOLEAN. Once
                 `claimed` has three values the claim is graded and this member covers
                 two shapes without changing meaning: `none` answering at all, and
                 `partial` answering the whole question. See Claimed.

      unclaimed  `claimed` is `none` and the witness said cannotAsk. Quiet, and correct.
                 ⚠️ `partial` with cannotAsk is NOT this: the witness took on part of
                 the question and then delivered nothing, which is `diverged`.

      ⚠️ Verdict is NOT the absence vocabulary. `pm:AbsenceReason` says why a DOCUMENT
      has no value; this says how an ANSWERER stood against a question. They rhyme
      and they are different axes; merging them is the error this project keeps
      catching one level down.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="agreed"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="diverged"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="notable"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="unclaimed"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       CITATION: a question's or an answer's authority, named.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Citation">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      WHICH CLAUSE, IN WHICH VERSION, DECIDED THIS.

      `instrument` is the standard or pack, as a BorrowedTerm so the naming authority
      is explicit. `clause` is the part of it: "606-10-25-1", "§4.2". `version` is
      the edition READ, because a standard is not a fixed object and a citation
      without a year is a citation to whatever is current.

      ⛔ THIS IS THE FIELD MOST LIKELY TO BE LEFT EMPTY AND MOST EXPENSIVE WHEN IT IS.
      A corpus whose questions cite nothing cannot be checked against the instrument
      it claims to test; it can only be checked against itself. Optional here because
      a required element that senders cannot fill produces invented values, but a
      claim with no citation is weaker, and a receiver is entitled to say so.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="instrument" type="pm:BorrowedTerm"/>
      <xs:element name="clause"     type="xs:token"  minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="version"    type="xs:token"  minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       COVERAGE: the claim. One witness, one corpus, question by question.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="CoverageEntry">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      `key` names a question in the corpus this coverage answers. ⚠️ Keys are the
      corpus's, and this schema cannot validate them; a runner must, and refusing a
      claim about a question the corpus does not hold is what stops a coverage file
      drifting into fiction.

      `claimed` is HOW MUCH of the question the witness says it answers. ⭐ It is what
      makes `notable` reachable: without a claim there is nothing for an answer to
      exceed, and a trial that cannot produce `notable` is only checking that a program
      agrees with itself. ⛔ It was an `xs:boolean` named `complete` and the third value
      it could not hold is `partial`; see Claimed.

      ⭐⭐ `answer` IS OPTIONAL, AND WHICH WAY IT IS FILLED SAYS WHAT KIND OF WITNESS
      THIS IS:

        absent   the witness is a PROGRAM. Run it; this file only says what it
                 claims, and the answer comes from the run.
        present  ⭐ THE WITNESS IS THE FILE. An accountant answering the corpus on
                 paper, or a regime nobody has written code for yet, states the
                 answer here and needs no runner at all. This is how a jurisdiction
                 arrives before an implementation of it does.

      `exception` is the reason this witness's answer differs from the corpus ON
      PURPOSE, and it is only meaningful beside an `answer`. ⛔ XSD 1.0 cannot express
      "only with", so a runner must refuse the combination. AND AN UNEXPLAINED
      DIVERGENCE IS NOT CONFORMANT: a difference with no reason is indistinguishable
      from a bug somebody stopped chasing.

      ⚠️ A regime that genuinely disagrees with the corpus is THE FINDING. A regime
      that quietly rewrites the corpus to agree with itself is a fork.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="key"       type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="claimed"   type="asrt:Claimed"/>
      <xs:element name="answer"    type="asrt:Answer"   minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="exception" type="xs:string"   minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="citation"  type="asrt:Citation" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Coverage">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ A WITNESS IS NOT REQUIRED TO BE A PROGRAM. When an accountant answers the
      corpus on paper, their answers ARE a coverage file, and that is the reason this
      is a document format rather than a Rust trait. A second regime should be able to
      answer without running, compiling or depending on anything of ours.

      `regime` is `pm:Regime`, reused rather than re-stated: a witness reports under a
      framework exactly the way a document does, and the same three-axis trap applies
      (jurisdiction · framework · the authority coding it). ⭐ TWO REGIMES ARE
      LEGITIMATE HERE FOR THE SAME REASON THEY ARE THERE: one witness may answer
      under a framework coded by two authorities that do not line up.

      ⚠️ `regime` is optional, and its absence is a real state rather than an
      oversight: a witness that is not an accounting model (a register census, a
      spreadsheet-formula checker) reports under no framework and should not be made
      to invent one.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness" type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"  type="pm:Regime"      minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="entry"   type="asrt:CoverageEntry" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       RUN: a trial, promoted to evidence.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="Result">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      One question, one witness, one verdict, and where it matters, what the
      witness actually said.

      ⭐ `answer` is here as well as on a coverage entry because they are different
      facts: there, it is what the witness CLAIMS; here, it is what the run OBSERVED.
      A promoted run whose answers were copied from the claim would be evidence of
      nothing, and keeping both is what makes `diverged` checkable after the fact.

      `evidence` is the human-readable trace, the line a reader follows to satisfy
      themselves the verdict is real. ⛔ A `diverged` with no evidence is an assertion
      about the past that nobody can check, which is the state a promoted record
      exists to prevent.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="key"      type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="verdict"  type="asrt:Verdict"/>
      <xs:element name="answer"   type="asrt:Answer" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="evidence" type="xs:string"   minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Run">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔ THIS IS THE PROMOTED RECORD, NOT THE LEDGER. A runner accumulates results
      locally, forever, on one machine; that file is a working record and a claim
      made from it is a claim about one machine. THIS is what you get when a report
      argues from a run: an extract, dated, that travels with the argument.

      ⭐ `ranAt` is REQUIRED, and it is the difference between the two. A working
      ledger's dates are per-line and internal; a promoted record is a statement
      about a moment, and a promotion with no date cannot be compared to the next
      one, which is the entire reason to promote it.

      ⚠️ `corpusVersion` names WHICH corpus was answered. A verdict is meaningless
      against an unnamed corpus: the questions change, and "it went green" without
      saying what it was asked is not a result.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness"       type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="ranAt"         type="xs:date"/>
      <xs:element name="corpusVersion" type="xs:token"    minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="result"        type="asrt:Result" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="run" type="asrt:Run">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐ A run promoted to evidence. ⛔ NOT a runner's working ledger. See the type.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:key name="resultKey">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:result"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:key"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

  <xs:element name="coverage" type="asrt:Coverage">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      One witness's claim about one corpus. ⛔ A coverage file with no entries is an
      error rather than a witness that claims nothing: a corpus of nothing runs no
      trials and reports success, which is how a green suite comes to mean nothing.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:key name="entryKey">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:key"/>
    </xs:key>
    <xs:key name="regimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:regime"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       DEPENDENCE: an observed dependence between TWO FILINGS, filed by
       somebody who is the filer of neither.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:complexType name="FiledLayer">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ONE END: WHOSE FILING, WHICH FILING, WHICH EDITION OF IT, AND WHERE IN IT.

      ⭐⭐ FOUR FACTS, AND THAT IS WHY THIS IS A TYPE RATHER THAN A STRING. "Acme Sub's
      labour layer in the 2026-Q2 stack" is a party, a document, an edition and a
      position inside it. One token carrying all four is joinable on none of them, and a
      receiver holding fifty of these cannot ask "everything touching Acme Sub" without
      matching text.

      `filing` is `pm:ForeignId`, used exactly as its own annotation describes it: "a
      notation plus an id names a POSITION IN ONE FILE that no authority publishes". The
      notation is the URI of the OTHER FILING; the id is the layer name inside it. ⭐ It
      is that type's second use and its first as a reference TARGET rather than a field,
      and it needed no widening: a layer name is precisely a position no authority
      publishes, which is why `pm:ForeignId` and not `pm:BorrowedTerm`.

      ⛔⛔ NOTHING HERE VALIDATES THAT THE FILING EXISTS, THAT THE URI RESOLVES, OR THAT
      THE LAYER IS IN IT. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. An XSD identity constraint is
      scoped to one document, and a keyref that appeared to reach across one would be the
      exact defect this document type exists to avoid: a reference that looks constrained
      and is not. An implementer that can fetch the other filing still owes the check; one
      that cannot owes the reader the knowledge that it did not happen.

      `party` is REQUIRED. ⭐ Without it two ends could be two filings of ONE entity and a
      reader could not tell, which silently loses the fact this whole document type exists
      for: THE TWO ENDS CANNOT SEE EACH OTHER.

      `registration` is that party in a register that publishes one (an LEI, a national
      tax number, an EUID) as a `pm:BorrowedTerm`, so the register is NAMED and two
      spellings of one name become joinable rather than undetectable. It is S-3's argument
      about signers, applied to entities. ⚠️ Optional, and deliberately unwrapped: the term
      is an ADDITION to the identity rather than the FRAME it is read under, so its silence
      says only "not cited". ⛔ Required would be wrong at exactly the end of the chain this
      model cares about (the small supplier with no register entry), and would produce
      invented identifiers.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND A NOTATION MAY BE THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF LOCAL
      COMPOSITION. `pm:processModulus/notation` lets a filing say which filing it is, so a part
      whose notation matches the embedded filing's own is a LOCAL part: it names a layer this
      composer built, in this document. ⛔ There is no second kind of part and no new element —
      the distinction is a string comparison against the filing's own name.

      ⭐⭐ WHAT IT BUYS IS THE CONSTRUCTION A THIRD PARTY ACTUALLY PERFORMS: bring their layer
      in, make your own layer beside it, then make a THIRD layer out of both — and separate all
      three afterwards. Every step but the last was already possible; the last needed only a
      filing that could name itself.

      ⚠️ AND THE ALTERNATION IT ENABLES IS AN ORDINARY FUSION, NOT A NEW RELATION. Two views of
      one quantity summed is that quantity COUNTED TWICE, which is exactly what `Elimination`
      removes — see `EliminationAgainst/demand`. The fused figure is the sum less an
      elimination equal to one view, the sum rule stays exact, and nothing new was needed.

      ⛔⛔ TWO RULES ARRIVE WITH IT AND NEITHER IS REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. A local part's `id`
      must name a layer in this document's own stack — XSD 1.0 cannot make a keyref conditional
      on another field's value, so `composedLayerName` cannot be pointed at it. And LOCAL PARTS
      MUST NOT CYCLE: layer A composed from B while B is composed from A is a document with no
      arithmetic in it at all. `assets/sql/rules.sql` already walks the descent with a depth
      guard and is where both are checked.

      `version` is THE EDITION OF THE FILING THAT WAS READ, and it is `Citation/version`'s
      argument applied to a filing rather than a standard: a stack is not a fixed object,
      and a reference with no edition is a reference to whatever is current. ⚠️ It sits
      BESIDE `filing` rather than inside it because `pm:ForeignId` is shared with the base
      schema, and a document type does not get to widen a shared type for its own
      convenience.

      `regime` is a DOCUMENT-LOCAL HANDLE naming which of THIS statement's declared regimes
      that filing reported under. ⭐ It is the one reference in this type a validator can
      check, and it IS checked. See the keyrefs on the element. ⚠️ It is not a copy of the
      other filing's declaration presented as that entity's own fact: the observer RESTATES
      it, dated by `observedAt`, which is `Holder`'s answer to a cached value nothing
      refreshes.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="party"        type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="registration" type="pm:BorrowedTerm" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="filing"       type="pm:ForeignId"/>
      <xs:element name="version"      type="xs:token" minOccurs="0"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"       type="xs:token" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="DependenceEntry">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ONE PAIR, AND WHAT WAS SEEN.

      `observed` is REQUIRED, and the sentence is `pm:Coupling`'s carried over word for
      word on purpose: A COUPLING FILED WITHOUT SAYING WHAT WAS OBSERVED IS AN OPINION, and
      the one element whose purpose is to carry a refutation is the last place to accept
      one. ⛔⛔ IT IS LESS NEGOTIABLE HERE, NOT MORE. There, a reader who doubts the
      observation can read the rest of the filing it sits in. Here BOTH ENDS ARE ELSEWHERE,
      so this element is the entire evidence a receiver holds.

      ⛔ AND IT IS NOT ABSENT-ABLE. A typed absence on `observed` would be an opinion
      wearing a type, strictly worse than an untyped one, because it would look conformant.

      `strength` is `pm:StatedClaim` and OPTIONAL, identical to `pm:Coupling/strength`, for
      the reason `Remainder/sign` gives: THE DIRECTION IS FREQUENTLY KNOWN WHEN THE MAGNITUDE
      IS NOT. "The subsidiary ran hot whenever the parent cut its block" is a filable
      observation with no number in it. ⚠️ Requiring it here was considered and DECLINED: the
      two elements carry the SAME FACT, and holding the cross-filing one to a stricter
      standard would say the asymmetry between them is about MEASUREMENT when it is about
      ATTESTATION. If `pm:Coupling/strength` is ever promoted to required, this moves with it.

      ⭐ `from` AND `to` ARE DIRECTED, AND THE DIRECTION IS A CLAIM. "Relieving the supplier's
      quantum reduced the customer's remainder" is a different statement from its reverse, and
      an observer who files the wrong one has filed a wrong thing rather than a vague one.
      ⚠️ An observer who has comovement and NOT direction has a weaker observation and should
      say so in `observed` rather than picking an order quietly. If that turns out to be the
      common case, two unordered ends plus a typed direction is the change to argue, on
      evidence, and not here.

      ⛔ `from` AND `to` MUST NOT BE THE SAME FILING AND THE SAME LAYER. A layer is not
      coupled to itself. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR. XSD 1.0 has no `xs:assert` and
      cannot compare across elements. An implementer still owes this check; the schema cannot
      take it from them.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="from"     type="asrt:FiledLayer"/>
      <xs:element name="to"       type="asrt:FiledLayer"/>
      <xs:element name="observed" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="strength" type="pm:StatedClaim" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Dependence">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ AN OBSERVED DEPENDENCE BETWEEN TWO LAYERS THAT ARE FILED SEPARATELY, AND WHAT
      MAKES IT A DOCUMENT RATHER THAN AN ELEMENT IS ATTESTATION, NOT SHAPE.

      `pm:Coupling` is the same phenomenon inside one filing, and it exists so the model can
      be refuted in its own format. But the dependence that matters most is frequently
      between TWO ENTITIES THAT FILE SEPARATELY AND CANNOT SEE EACH OTHER: a parent and a
      subsidiary, a supplier and a customer, two borrowers of one lender.

      ⛔⛔ AND THE OBVIOUS REPAIR IS THE WRONG ONE, RECORDED HERE SO IT IS NOT RE-PROPOSED.
      Widening `pm:Coupling/from` and `/to` to accept a `pm:ForeignId` was considered and
      REJECTED. A FILING IS ATTESTABLE BY THE PARTY THAT FILES IT, and a coupling inside
      entity A's document naming entity B's layer is a claim A CANNOT ATTEST TO, because A
      cannot see B's stack. ⛔ WORSE: `couplingFrom` and `couplingTo` could not follow it, SO
      IT WOULD VALIDATE BY NOT BEING CHECKED: a reference that looks constrained and is not,
      while the two keyrefs went on reporting success about the half they could still reach.
      ⚠️ The two workarounds available before this element are worse again: fabricating one
      `Stack` spanning both entities asserts a shared system that does not exist and that
      neither filer could attest to, and a stub layer files a layer nobody has.

      ⭐⭐ SO THE THIRD PARTY FILES IT, AND THIS SCHEMA ALREADY HAS THAT GENRE. `Coverage` and
      `Run` are claims ABOUT things rather than elements INSIDE them, and each carries a
      `witness`. A group consolidator, a statutory auditor, a lender reading two borrowers:
      each has read both filings, NEITHER FILER HAS, and the observation is theirs to stand
      behind. ⭐ The world already files it this way: a consolidation is a separate
      statement, not a footnote in the subsidiary's accounts.

      ⭐⭐ BOTH ENDS ARE FOREIGN, ALWAYS, AND THAT IS NOT A LIMITATION. IT IS WHAT MAKES THE
      DESIGN WORK. This document is inside neither filing, so an auditor's own client's stack
      is as external to THIS statement as the counterparty's. There is never one local end
      beside one foreign one, and therefore never a keyref that must reach across a boundary
      and cannot. Every reference here is either wholly local and checked (the regime handles)
      or wholly foreign and honestly unchecked (the filings).

      ⚠️ A WITNESS WHO IS THE FILER OF BOTH ENDS IS IN THE WRONG DOCUMENT. Their own
      observation belongs in `pm:Coupling`, inside the filing they attest to. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE
      BY A VALIDATOR: nothing here knows who filed what. ⭐ But `from` and `to` MAY name the
      same filing: an auditor who sees a coupling between two layers of one client's stack has
      an observation the client did not file and cannot be made to file. The distinguishing
      fact is WHO OBSERVED, not where the ends are.

      `witness` is REQUIRED, exactly as on `Coverage` and `Run`, and for a sharper reason than
      either: THIS IS TESTIMONY ABOUT PARTIES WHO DID NOT CONSENT TO IT, and anonymous
      testimony about somebody else's books is not a filing.

      ⭐ `observedAt` is REQUIRED, and it is `Run/ranAt`'s argument rather than `Coverage`'s. A
      coverage claim is a standing position; THIS IS A STATEMENT ABOUT A MOMENT. Both filings
      change, so an observation that does not say when it was made cannot be checked against
      the filings as they were, nor compared with the next one, which is the entire reason to
      file it. ⚠️ One date and not an interval, for `Jagged`'s stated reason: time is an axis
      on a simulation of this model, not a field on its types.

      `provenance` is where a firm's own chain goes: who entered, who approved, and above all
      `standing`, which is the axis separating a statutory auditor's observation from a
      controller's hunch, and which is a BorrowedTerm so neither this schema nor any witness
      owns that set. ⚠️ Optional, because `witness` already forbids silence and every field
      inside `Provenance` is itself optional, so requiring the container would buy an empty
      tag. ⚠️ `witness` AND `provenance/party` ARE NOT ONE FACT WITH TWO HOMES: the first is
      the observer as named across this file's documents, the second is the entity standing
      behind it, and they differ whenever the observer is a team or a tool inside a firm, the
      same split `Holder` draws between kind and party.

      `regime` is `pm:Regime`, unbounded and optional, exactly as on `Coverage`, and it is the
      frameworks THIS OBSERVER computed remainders under. ⭐⭐ IT IS ALSO WHAT MAKES TWO ENDS
      UNDER DIFFERENT FRAMEWORKS LEGIBLE, WHICH IS THE CONSOLIDATOR'S ORDINARY CASE: a
      Portuguese subsidiary under NCRF-PE and a US parent under US-GAAP measure a remainder
      differently, and a document that cannot say so has hidden the reason the two numbers
      disagree. Each end names WHICH declared regime its filing reported under. ⛔ THE
      ALTERNATIVE, declaring both and explaining which is which inside `observed`, IS THE
      ERROR `Absence` ALREADY NAMES: a reason no query can reach is not a typed absence, and a
      regime attribution no query can reach is not an attribution.

      `citation` names the instrument under which this observer could see BOTH filings: a
      group-audit standard, a consolidation standard, an engagement. ⚠️ It is the nearest
      thing to an answer to "why should a reader believe you saw both?", which is the question
      this document type raises and no other element answers. Optional for `Citation`'s own
      stated reason: a required element senders cannot fill produces invented values. A
      dependence with no citation is weaker, and a receiver is entitled to say so.

      ⚠️ ONE STATEMENT, ONE MOMENT, MANY OBSERVATIONS. A consolidator reading a group files
      one document with an entry per pair, the way a run files a result per question.
      Observations made on different days are different statements, for the same reason a
      promotion is dated.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness"    type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="observedAt" type="xs:date"/>
      <xs:element name="provenance" type="pm:Provenance"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"     type="pm:Regime"            minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="citation"   type="asrt:Citation"        minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="entry"      type="asrt:DependenceEntry"               maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="dependence" type="asrt:Dependence">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐ ONE OBSERVER'S STATEMENT THAT TWO SEPARATELY FILED STACKS MOVE TOGETHER.

      ⛔ NOT `pm:Coupling`, AND NOT `pm:Coupling` WIDENED. See the type. A coupling belongs
      inside the filing whose filer can attest to both ends; this exists for the case where
      NOBODY WHO CAN FILE IT CAN ATTEST TO IT.

      ⛔ A dependence document with no entries is an error rather than an observer who
      observed nothing. An empty statement about two filings reports success at having
      looked, which is how a document that means nothing comes to be cited.

      ⭐ THE ONLY REFERENCES CHECKED HERE ARE THE ONES THAT CAN BE. The regime handles are
      document-local and a stock XSD 1.0 validator resolves them. ⛔ THE FILINGS ARE NOT AND
      CANNOT BE: that is the whole reason this is a separate document, and pretending
      otherwise with a keyref would reintroduce the defect it was created to avoid.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>

    <!-- ⚠️ NOT `regimeId`: identity-constraint names share one symbol space per target
         namespace, and `asrt:coverage` already declares one. -->
    <xs:key name="dependenceRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:regime"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>

    <xs:keyref name="fromRegime" refer="asrt:dependenceRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry/asrt:from"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:regime"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <xs:keyref name="toRegime" refer="asrt:dependenceRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry/asrt:to"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:regime"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <!-- One directed pair, one entry. Two observations about one pair by one witness on one
         date belong in one entry whose `observed` says both things; otherwise `strength`
         has no domain. A→B and B→A are different sequences and both stay legal: a mutual
         dependence is a legitimate filing. -->
    <xs:key name="dependencePair">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:entry"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:from/asrt:filing/pm:notation"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:from/asrt:filing/pm:id"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:to/asrt:filing/pm:notation"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:to/asrt:filing/pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       COMPOSITION: one filing assembled OUT OF other filings, by the party
       with standing to assemble it.
       ================================================================== -->
  <xs:simpleType name="EliminationAgainst">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      WHICH OF THE THREE QUANTITIES AN ELIMINATION IS REMOVED FROM.

      ⭐⭐ THE SET IS NOT A JUDGEMENT CALL: it is `pm:Layer`'s own three quantities, and
      that annotation already says why keeping them apart is the entire point. An
      elimination that does not say which one it hits is an adjustment applied to
      whichever number the reader happened to be holding.

        demand      what was ASKED, counted twice. ⭐ THE ORDINARY CASE, and the one that
                    surprises people: when one member commissions work from another, BOTH
                    file it as their own demand, honestly, and the group's demand is the
                    sum MINUS the commission.
        nameplate   what was COMMITTED, counted twice. ⚠️ RARER THAN IT LOOKS, and the
                    asymmetry is the interesting part: two members' people are two sets of
                    people, so labour nameplate almost never eliminates. A reservation one
                    member holds and resells to another does.
        draw        what was actually SERVED, counted twice. Present because the model
                    says these are three quantities and not two; an implementation that
                    eliminates demand and forgets the draw has silently claimed a
                    utilisation it did not observe.

      ⭐ CONTRIBUTED, and closed, because this model owns the distinction. It is not a
      chart of accounts and no accounting framework publishes it: a consolidation standard
      eliminates BALANCES, and these are not balances.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:restriction base="xs:token">
      <xs:enumeration value="demand"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="nameplate"/>
      <xs:enumeration value="draw"/>
    </xs:restriction>
  </xs:simpleType>

  <xs:complexType name="Elimination">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ WHAT THE COMPOSER REMOVED, AND WHY THE FUSED FIGURE IS THEREFORE NOT THE SUM
      OF ITS PARTS. Without this element a composition can only be one of two useless
      things: a restatement forced to equal the sum, which no real consolidation is, or a
      figure that differs from the sum for reasons nobody recorded.

      ⛔⛔ AND THAT IS WHY THE SUM RULE COULD NOT BE WRITTEN BEFORE THIS TYPE EXISTED.
      A checker comparing the fused figure against the sum of the parts has no way to tell
      an elimination from an error, so the strongest thing it could report was a warning.
      With eliminations filed the rule becomes exact, and any leftover difference is a
      finding rather than a shrug.

      `quantity` is a `pm:StatedClaim` AND NOT A BARE `pm:Claim`, which is the decision in
      this type most worth keeping. ⭐ "There is intra-group demand here and we cannot size
      it" IS THE HONEST STATE OF MOST CONSOLIDATIONS, and a required number would be
      answered by writing zero. An `unmeasured` elimination says the composer looked, found
      double counting, and could not measure it — and a receiver reading that knows the
      fused figure is overstated by an unknown amount, which is strictly more than silence.
      ⚠️ `none` HERE IS NARROWER THAN IT WAS, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT USED TO STAND IN THIS
      SPOT HAS MOVED UP A LEVEL. It said "`none` is NOT the same as filing no elimination at
      all: the first says somebody looked, the second says nothing" — correct, and about the
      SEARCH rather than about any one elimination, which is why it is now `StatedEliminations`
      and why `Fusion/eliminations` is required. What `none` means on THIS element is that a
      named pair has a double-counting relation which sizes to zero this period. ⛔ Filing one
      against an arbitrary pair to mean "we found nothing" invents a relation.

      ⭐⭐ ELIMINATIONS SUBTRACT COMPONENT-WISE AND DO NOT REVERSE BOUNDS, WHICH IS THE
      OPPOSITE OF `pm:Remainder`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS NOT AN INCONSISTENCY. A remainder
      subtracts demand from nameplate, two INDEPENDENT quantities, so the worst case of the
      difference pairs one's low against the other's high and the bounds reverse. An
      elimination subtracts a COMPONENT OF THE VERY FIGURE IT IS REMOVED FROM: in the
      scenario where total demand is low, the intra-group part of it is low too. Reversing
      bounds here would widen an interval by pairing a scenario against itself.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR, and it is the check most likely to be got wrong
      quietly, because the wrong answer is still a well-formed interval.

      `against` is REQUIRED. See EliminationAgainst.

      `observed` is REQUIRED, for `pm:Coupling/observed`'s reason and a sharper one: this
      is the only element in the model that makes a filed number smaller, and an unexplained
      reduction is the shape every accounting scandal has in common.

      `between` names the filed layers the double counting runs between, so the attribution
      is QUERYABLE rather than narrated. ⚠️ Optional and unbounded, and deliberately NOT
      constrained to this fusion's own parts: a group eliminating against a member that files
      nothing, or against a layer folded into a different composed layer, is ordinary, and a
      keyref that forced the subset would make the honest case unfileable.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="quantity" type="pm:StatedClaim"/>
      <xs:element name="against"  type="asrt:EliminationAgainst"/>
      <xs:element name="observed" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="between"  type="asrt:FiledLayer" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Part">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ONE FILED LAYER GOING INTO A FUSION, AND THE FACTOR THAT PUTS IT IN THE COMPOSED
      LAYER'S UNIT.

      ⛔⛔ THE CASE THIS EXISTS FOR IS METERING, NOT TRANSLATION. `Fusion` argues correctly
      that the units of its parts must NOT be compared as strings, because `people` and
      `pessoas` are one unit spelled twice. That argument does not reach a group whose members
      METER THE SAME RESOURCE DIFFERENTLY — one billing per reserved card and another per
      card-hour, one per tonne and another per truckload. `4.4 GPU + 545 GPU-hour` is not a
      sum, and a composer who silently multiplies by 720 has done exactly the unaudited
      consolidation arithmetic `Elimination` was added to stop. ⭐ The likeliest instance is
      not exotic at all: it is a TIME WINDOW, a member filing weekly into a group that files
      monthly.

      ⭐ `factor` SITS BESIDE `FiledLayer` AND NEVER INSIDE IT, for the reason already
      recorded at `FiledLayer/version`: that type is shared with `dependence`, and a document
      type does not get to widen a shared type for its own convenience. A dependence end has
      no composed unit to convert into.

      ⛔ ABSENT MEANS ONE, EXACTLY, AND NEVER "UNKNOWN". The overwhelming majority of parts
      need no conversion and must not be made to file a ceremonial 1. A conversion nobody has
      measured is `absent reason="unmeasured"` INSIDE the `StatedClaim`, which is a different
      document from a part with no `factor` element at all, and that difference is the whole
      reason `Stated*` exists.

      ⭐ `factor` IS A CLAIM RATHER THAN A NUMBER BECAUSE A CONVERSION IS ITSELF UNCERTAIN.
      "One truckload is 22 tonnes" and "a month is 720 hours" are three-point claims like
      everything else here; hours in a month is `[672, 720, 744]` and pretending otherwise
      files a precision nobody has.

      ⛔ THE PRODUCT IS COMPONENT-WISE, AND IT IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE A FACTOR IS STRICTLY
      POSITIVE. `low·low`, `mostLikely·mostLikely`, `high·high`. Interval multiplication in
      general must consider four corner products, because a factor spanning zero reorders the
      bounds — a conversion never does, so the general case is not owed here and must not be
      assumed available elsewhere. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR.

      ⛔⛔ A CONVERTED PART'S REMAINDER IS CONVERTED DIRECTLY AND NEVER RE-DERIVED FROM ITS
      CONVERTED NAMEPLATE AND DEMAND, AND THE TWO GENUINELY DISAGREE. One factor multiplies
      BOTH the nameplate and the demand of the same part, so those two converted intervals are
      correlated; subtracting them with the bound reversal that INDEPENDENT quantities require
      counts the factor's own spread twice. In the corpus this reads
      `[1092.0, 2857.0, 4198.8]` re-derived against `[1414.0, 2857.0, 4085.6]` converted, and
      they agree only at `mostLikely`. ⭐ It is the same correlated-components question
      `Elimination` had to answer, arriving in a second place with the same answer.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR.

      ⚠️ AN ELIMINATION IS STATED AFTER CONVERSION, in the composed layer's unit. Parts arrive
      in whatever their filers used; the sum they are removed from does not.

      ⚠️ AND THE MODEL DOES NOT KNOW THAT TWO PARTS' FACTORS ARE THE SAME FACTOR. Two members
      converting through one month have one real uncertainty filed twice, and a composer who
      lets them disagree has published a document whose arithmetic nobody downstream can make
      consistent. That consistency is the composer's, and nothing here takes it from them.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="layer"  type="asrt:FiledLayer"/>
      <xs:element name="factor" type="pm:StatedClaim" minOccurs="0"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="StatedEliminations">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⛔⛔⛔ DID THE COMPOSER LOOK FOR DOUBLE COUNTING? An unbounded `elimination` could
      not answer it, and `Elimination`'s own annotation shows the model trying to answer
      it one level too low: "`none` remains meaningful and is NOT the same as filing no
      elimination at all: the first says somebody looked, the second says nothing."

      ⭐ THAT SENTENCE IS RIGHT ABOUT THE DISTINCTION AND WAS PUTTING IT IN THE WRONG
      PLACE. "Somebody looked and found none" is a fact about the SEARCH, and a search
      has no `against` and no `between` — so filing it as an Elimination forced a
      composer to name a quantity and a pair of layers for a relation they are asserting
      does not exist. It belongs on the list, and it is here now.

        elimination+                somebody looked and found these
        absent reason="none"        ⭐ SOMEBODY LOOKED AND THE PARTS DO NOT DOUBLE COUNT.
                                    The fused figure IS the sum, asserted rather than
                                    assumed
        absent reason="unmeasured"  nobody checked. The fused figure may be overstated
                                    by an unknown amount and the composer says so
        absent reason="notApplicable"  a one-part fusion. Nothing can be counted twice
                                    between a set of one

      ⭐⭐ AND IT RESCUES THE SUM RULE FROM ITS OWN LOOPHOLE. `Elimination` argues that
      filed eliminations make the rule EXACT rather than a warning. That holds for a
      fusion that files one; for a fusion that files none the checker was back to a
      warning and did not know it, because an empty list and a checked-clean list are
      the same bytes. Under `none` the rule is exact again: `Σ parts` must equal the
      composed figure, full stop. Under `unmeasured` it is explicitly SUSPENDED, which
      is what the unsized-elimination case already does one level down.

      ⚠️ WHAT SURVIVES OF THE INNER `none`, AND IT IS NARROWER THAN IT WAS. An
      `elimination` whose `quantity` is `absent reason="none"` now says something
      specific: THIS NAMED PAIR of layers has a double-counting relation and it sizes to
      zero this period. That is a real filing — a commission arrangement that existed all
      year and was not exercised — and it is not the same as the search finding nothing.
      ⛔ A composer who means "no double counting anywhere" and files a zero elimination
      against an arbitrary pair has invented a relation, which is the error typed absence
      exists to prevent, committed with a typed absence.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:choice>
      <xs:element name="elimination" type="asrt:Elimination" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="absent"      type="pm:Absence"/>
    </xs:choice>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Fusion">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ ONE COMPOSED LAYER, AND WHICH FILED LAYERS WENT INTO IT.

      ⛔⛔ NOT A COUPLING, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE REASON THIS TYPE EXISTS RATHER THAN A
      SECOND VERB ON `pm:Coupling`. A coupling is PEER TO PEER: two layers stay two, and one
      layer's remainder moves with the other's. A fusion is PART TO WHOLE: the parts are
      added and the composed layer is what they are parts of. ⭐ The tell is that fused
      layers need not be coupled at all — a US delivery team and a Portuguese one serve
      different customers in different countries and neither one's remainder moves when the
      other hires, yet they are one layer of the group. Same KIND, not same INSTANCE.

      ⭐⭐ AND THE MODEL'S OWN DEFINITION OF A LAYER DECIDES WHEN TO FUSE, WITH NOTHING
      ADDED. `pm:Layer` says a layer is a place where a remainder can be held INDEPENDENTLY
      of every other layer. So:

      > FUSE ONLY WHAT IS FUNGIBLE.

      If a unit of supply in one part can serve demand in another, the parts do not hold
      their remainders independently and they are one layer. If it cannot, THEY ARE TWO
      LAYERS, they belong in the stack as two, and a `pm:Coupling` between them is where any
      observed interaction goes. ⛔ Fusing what is not fungible is exactly the error
      `pm:Layer`'s fourth falsifier warns about, run backwards, and it produces a group
      remainder smaller than reality by letting one member's excess cover another's
      shortfall across a boundary nothing can cross.

      ⚠️ THE FUNGIBILITY JUDGEMENT IS NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR AND NEVER WILL BE. It is
      the composer's, it is what `observed` carries, and it is the claim a reader is entitled
      to disagree with. That is the point: it is now written down where it can be disagreed
      with, instead of being implicit in an arithmetic somebody performed once.

      ⭐ ONE PART IS A LEGITIMATE FUSION AND NOT A DEGENERATE ONE. It says the composer read
      that filed layer, decided it fuses with nothing, and carried it through — frequently
      under a different name. ⛔⛔ THAT IS PRECISELY WHAT STOPS THE NAME COLLISION FROM
      BECOMING A SILENT MERGE: two members can both file a layer called `compute` that are
      not the same layer, and nothing in either filing says so. The composer says so, by
      filing two one-part fusions under two names, and `observed` is REQUIRED so they have to
      say WHY. A heuristic joining on the token would have merged them and reported success.

      ⭐ A LAYER IN THE COMPOSED STACK WITH NO FUSION AT ALL IS THE THIRD STATE: one the
      composer ORIGINATED. A group-level rota, a shared function, an elimination entry that
      is a layer in its own right. Nothing here forces every composed layer to have come from
      somewhere, because a parent is an entity too.

      ⛔ THE FUSED FIGURE IS NOT CHECKED AGAINST THE PARTS. `Σ parts - Σ eliminations`
      against the composed layer's own claim, per quantity, is arithmetic over documents that
      are not this one. ⭐ `eliminations` IS REQUIRED so that a reader knows WHICH ARITHMETIC
      is owed: under `absent reason="none"` the composed figure must equal `Σ parts` exactly,
      under `unmeasured` no equality is owed at all, and an empty list used to mean either.
      See StatedEliminations. ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR: an implementer that can fetch the
      parts owes the check, and one that cannot owes the reader the knowledge that it did not
      happen, exactly as for `FiledLayer`. ⚠️ Nor are the UNITS checked, and that limit is
      structural rather than lazy: the parts legitimately spell one unit two ways (`people`,
      `pessoas`) and asserting they are one unit IS WHAT THE FUSION SAYS. A checker demanding
      string equality would reject the case this type was built for.

      ⛔⛔ THAT ARGUMENT COVERS RENAMING AND DOES NOT COVER CONVERTING, AND THE TWO WERE
      CONFLATED UNTIL `Part/factor` SEPARATED THEM. `people` and `pessoas` are one unit spelled
      twice and a fusion asserts exactly that. `GPU` and `GPU-hour` are NOT, and a composer who
      adds them has performed the unaudited arithmetic `Elimination` exists to stop. See `Part`.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="name"        type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="part"        type="asrt:Part"        maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="observed"     type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="eliminations" type="asrt:StatedEliminations"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:complexType name="Composition">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐⭐⭐ A FILING ASSEMBLED OUT OF OTHER FILINGS, PLUS THE MAPPING SAYING WHAT CAME FROM
      WHERE. It is `Dependence`'s genre — a third party who read filings they did not make —
      carried one step further: this observer does not merely comment on the filings, THEY
      FILE.

      ⛔⛔ AND IT HAS TO ANSWER AN OBJECTION THIS SCHEMA ALREADY WROTE DOWN AGAINST ITSELF.
      `Dependence` rejects one workaround in these words: "fabricating one `Stack` spanning
      both entities asserts a shared system that does not exist and that NEITHER FILER COULD
      ATTEST TO." Every word of that stands. ⭐ The escape is the clause it turns on: neither
      FILER. A parent asserting that its members are one system is not fabricating a shared
      system, it is exercising the standing that makes it a parent, and the same annotation
      says so two paragraphs later — "a consolidation is a separate statement, not a footnote
      in the subsidiary's accounts."

      ⛔ SO THE CONDITION IS SHARP, AND A COMPOSITION FILED WITHOUT IT IS A FABRICATION: the
      composed stack must be THE COMPOSER'S OWN CLAIM, signed, and never a reconstruction of
      a system the members are asserted to already share. `witness` and `provenance/standing`
      are what separate the two.

      ⛔⛔⛔ AND THAT PARAGRAPH STOOD HERE FOR THREE REVISIONS WHILE `provenance` WAS
      `minOccurs="0"`. The schema named the condition that separates a filing from a
      fabrication and then permitted filings without it — which is `Coupling`'s shape a third
      time, and `Absence`'s argument turned on this schema's own header. It is REQUIRED now,
      and `Provenance/standing` is a required `StatedBorrowedTerm` beneath it, so a composer
      with no standing to declare says so rather than being silent about the one field that
      makes the document legitimate.

      ⭐ WHY IT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN ANYWHERE ELSE: what a layer in this stack MEANS depends
      on who filed it. A parent's composition asserts a group's system; an auditor's asserts
      what an auditor holds. The same layer in the two documents is not the same claim, and
      the standing is the only thing that says which you are reading. They are the same fields
      for the same reason as on `Coverage`, `Run` and `Dependence`.

      ⭐⭐ IT EMBEDS A WHOLE `pm:processModulus`, NOT A BARE `pm:stack`, AND THAT BUYS TWO
      THINGS NEITHER OBVIOUS NOR FREE. The composed filing is EXTRACTABLE BYTE FOR BYTE and
      readable by anything that reads the base schema with no knowledge of this one. And its
      own identity constraints — `layerName`, `couplingFrom`, `couplingTo`, `drawLayer`,
      `inducesLayer` — all validate inside its subtree, so the group's stack is checked
      exactly as a standalone filing is, including its couplings.

      ⭐⭐ AND `pm:Coupling` IS REACHABLE HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE IN A CONSOLIDATION. Its ends
      are keyrefs into one stack, so a composition that carried only a mapping could not
      express one. A parent observing that two of its composed layers move together has made
      an observation NEITHER MEMBER COULD MAKE and both are entitled to see.

      TWO REGIME DECLARATIONS, TWO DIFFERENT FACTS, and collapsing them loses the reason the
      numbers disagree:

        asrt:regime   what the MEMBERS reported under, RESTATED by this observer and dated
                      by `observedAt`. Each `part` names which. Identical in role to
                      `Dependence`'s, for the identical reason.
        pm:regime     inside the embedded filing: what THE CONSOLIDATION ITSELF reports
                      under, which is frequently neither member's framework.

      ⭐ IT RECURSES, WITH NOTHING ADDED. A composition contains a filing, and `FiledLayer`
      points at filings, so a segment composes members and a group composes segments. ⛔ The
      alternative — a mapping-only document carrying no figures — cannot recurse usefully,
      because the next level up would have to re-derive from the leaves every time and would
      have no edition to pin.

      ⚠️ WHERE A MAPPING-ONLY DOCUMENT WOULD STILL BE RIGHT, recorded so it is not mistaken
      for an oversight: a CROSSWALK, published by somebody with standing to observe that two
      layers are the same and NO standing to state anybody's group figures — a trade body
      across its members, a researcher across a sector. That document has a different witness
      test and would be a different root. It is not this one, and this one must not be filed
      in its place.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="witness"    type="xs:token"/>
      <xs:element name="observedAt" type="xs:date"/>
      <xs:element name="provenance" type="pm:Provenance"/>
      <xs:element name="regime"     type="pm:Regime"     minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element name="citation"   type="asrt:Citation" minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
      <xs:element ref="pm:processModulus"/>
      <xs:element name="fusion"     type="asrt:Fusion"   maxOccurs="unbounded"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>

  <xs:element name="composition" type="asrt:Composition">
    <xs:annotation><xs:documentation>
      ⭐ ONE PARENT'S FILING, ASSEMBLED FROM ITS MEMBERS' FILINGS, SAYING WHICH LAYERS IT
      TREATED AS ONE AND WHAT IT REMOVED TO DO SO.

      ⛔ A composition with no fusions is an error rather than a composer who composed
      nothing, for `coverage`'s reason: a document asserting a group stack while naming
      nothing it was built from is an unsourced filing that looks sourced.

      ⭐⭐ MORE IS CHECKED HERE THAN IN ANY OTHER CROSS-FILING DOCUMENT, AND THE REASON IS
      THAT ONE END IS LOCAL. `dependence` has two foreign ends and can check only its regime
      handles. A fusion has a foreign end and a LOCAL one — the composed layer is in this very
      document — so `fusionName` is a real keyref into the embedded stack. A fusion naming a
      layer the composer did not file is a schema error.

      ⭐⭐⭐ AND `partIdentity` IS A CONSOLIDATION RULE A VALIDATOR CAN ACTUALLY ENFORCE,
      which is rare enough here to be worth naming: ONE FILED LAYER CANNOT BE PART OF TWO
      COMPOSED LAYERS OF THIS DOCUMENT. Consolidating the same member layer into two places
      double counts it, and unlike almost everything else about a merge, both ends of that
      check are inside one document. ⚠️ It is keyed on the filing's notation and the layer
      name within it, which is `FiledLayer`'s position pair — NOT on `party`, since one
      entity may legitimately contribute many layers.

      ⛔⛔ THOSE FOUR WORDS — "OF THIS DOCUMENT" — ARE THE WHOLE QUALIFICATION, AND
      COMPOSITIONS NEST, SO THEY BITE. A composition is itself a filing, so a segment
      composes members and a group composes segments, with nothing added. At one level every
      part is a leaf and this key is therefore complete. AT TWO LEVELS IT IS NOT: a holding
      naming both `group#labour` and `member#labour` has two distinct notation/id pairs, the
      member's layer is consolidated twice — once directly and once through the group — and
      NOTHING HERE CATCHES IT. ⭐ The transitive rule is that NO LEAF LAYER IS REACHABLE
      THROUGH TWO PATHS, and it is owed by whoever can fetch the chain, exactly like the
      rule that a cited filing exists at all.
      ⛔ NOT REACHABLE BY A VALIDATOR, and the reason is not squeamishness: the second path
      runs through a document this one does not contain.

      ⚠️ ONE WRINKLE FOR A FETCHER, since nesting makes it real. A layer sits at
      `/pm:processModulus/pm:stack/pm:layer` in an ordinary filing and at
      `/asrt:composition/pm:processModulus/pm:stack/pm:layer` in a composition. `notation` is
      opaque and says which is which nowhere, so a resolver reads the root element to find
      out. ⭐ That is the price of embedding the filing whole rather than inlining a bare
      stack, and it buys the extractability and the inner keyrefs described on the type.

      ⛔ THE FILINGS THEMSELVES ARE STILL NOT VALIDATED. Same as `dependence`, same reason,
      and pretending otherwise with a keyref would reintroduce the defect both documents
      exist to avoid.
    </xs:documentation></xs:annotation>

    <!-- ⚠️ Identity-constraint names share one symbol space per target namespace; `coverage`
         and `dependence` have already spent `regimeId` and `dependenceRegimeId`. -->
    <xs:key name="compositionRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:regime"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>

    <xs:keyref name="partRegime" refer="asrt:compositionRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:fusion/asrt:part/asrt:layer"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:regime"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <xs:keyref name="eliminationBetweenRegime" refer="asrt:compositionRegimeId">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:fusion/asrt:eliminations/asrt:elimination/asrt:between"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:regime"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <!-- ⭐ The one reference in a cross-filing document that is genuinely local: the
         composed layer is in this file, so a fusion cannot name a layer nobody filed. -->
    <xs:key name="composedLayerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="pm:processModulus/pm:stack/pm:layer"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="pm:name"/>
    </xs:key>

    <xs:keyref name="fusionName" refer="asrt:composedLayerName">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:fusion"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:name"/>
    </xs:keyref>

    <!-- One composed layer, one fusion. Two fusions into one layer would be one fusion
         whose parts were split across two elements, and `elimination` would have no domain. -->
    <xs:key name="fusionTarget">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:fusion"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:name"/>
    </xs:key>

    <!-- ⭐⭐ No filed layer is consolidated twice. See the annotation. -->
    <xs:key name="partIdentity">
      <xs:selector xpath="asrt:fusion/asrt:part/asrt:layer"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:filing/pm:notation"/>
      <xs:field    xpath="asrt:filing/pm:id"/>
    </xs:key>
  </xs:element>

</xs:schema>
